From 06082a5ea506ab0746aa18501504a906e43b0480 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 19:49:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] The windows never resolved, and the sit played beside the
 chair

poh_dynamic_window shipped as a single loc whose model is a bare wall with a
window-shaped HOLE in it, and whose description read "This should have
resolved!". The templates place 116 of them per style, so every house has had
116 holes in its walls since the map import.

In OSRS it is a multiloc resolving to the window for your house style and the
glazing you have fitted, and all 54 windows were already in poh.loc - named
by town, which is what the cache calls the six styles. Matching town to style
by wall material, rendered side by side rather than guessed: rimmington is the
golden plank set that goes with village_wall, lumbridge the dark grey one that
goes with brickwall, and the desert, viking, timber and Yanille sets are
unmistakable.

%poh_window is style * 9 + choice over a 6-bit varbit, because a multiloc has
one multivar. A shell with no entry at the current value renders nothing and
carries no ops, so all 54 are listed and group 57 checks each child is the
window for the style its index claims - otherwise a stone house grows wooden
windows.

human_sitting_chair was always the right animation; four npcs carry it as a
readyanim. The problem was where it played. An oploc1 click walks the player
ADJACENT to the loc, so Sit-on left them standing beside an empty chair.
~poh_furn_sit walks onto the tile and faces the way the chair faces first.
377 still has no seated state, so it is a pose and not one the engine holds.

Also the audit: the templates place 183 hotspots and the spec builds on 77.
Of the other 106, thirteen are door hotspots that build mode already handles,
56 are ordinary buildable work with the art already sitting in poh.loc, and 33
need a dungeon or a second storey this build does not have.

rs2check 0 ERROR / 0 CHECK; poh_battery ALL PASS (57 groups);
poh_mutate 111/111 caught.

